  <main class="docs-main" id="main">
    <h1 class="docs-h1">Documentation</h1>
    <p class="docs-lede">Everything Downpick does, and the handful of decisions it asks you to make. Version {{ release.version }}.</p>

    <section id="getting-started" class="docs-section docs-section--first">
      <h2 class="docs-h2">Getting started</h2>
      <p class="docs-p docs-p--wide">Four steps from download to first result. Nothing to sign up for.</p>

      <div class="steps-list">
        <div class="numstep">
          <div class="numstep-n">01</div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="numstep-t">Install it</h3>
            <p class="numstep-b">Grab the build for your platform from the <a href="/download.html">download page</a>. On macOS, open the dmg and drag Downpick to Applications; on Windows, extract the zip anywhere and run <span class="mono">Downpick.exe</span>; on Linux, mark the AppImage executable and run it. The builds are unsigned, so the first launch needs one extra step — on macOS, System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → Open Anyway; on Windows, More info → Run anyway.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="numstep">
          <div class="numstep-n">02</div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="numstep-t">Set a master password</h3>
            <p class="numstep-b">The first launch asks you to choose one. It's the only password Downpick will ever ask for, and it unlocks everything else. Write it down somewhere safe — see <a href="#vault">the vault</a> for why. The same screen lets you say where the vault file goes, or open one you already have from another machine; leave it alone and it lands in your home directory.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="numstep">
          <div class="numstep-n">03</div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="numstep-t">Add a connection</h3>
            <p class="numstep-b">Press <span class="mono">+</span> at the top of the explorer. Pick the engine, then paste in the host, port, user, and password your database administrator gave you. On <a href="#oracle">Oracle</a> there's one extra field, the service name. Downpick tests the connection before saving it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="numstep">
          <div class="numstep-n">04</div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="numstep-t">Run a query</h3>
            <p class="numstep-b">Expand the connection, find a table, and double-click it — Downpick opens a tab with a query already written. Press <span class="mono" data-mac="Cmd+Enter" data-win="Ctrl+Enter">Cmd+Enter</span> to run it. Results appear below, with the row count and timing in the status bar.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="connections" class="docs-section">
      <h2 class="docs-h2">Connections</h2>
      <p class="docs-p">Each connection is one server — PostgreSQL, SQL Server, Oracle, or MongoDB. Add as many as you like — they all stay in the explorer, and more than one can be live at once. The dot beside the name is grey when idle and green when connected.</p>
      <p class="docs-p">The <span class="mono">⋮</span> menu on a connection edits it, disconnects it, or removes it. Removing a connection deletes its stored password from the vault too.</p>
      <p class="docs-p">On a connection that's already up, the same menu has <span class="strong-inline">Refresh databases</span>, which re-reads the server's database list for databases created or dropped outside Downpick. It leaves the connection and the schemas you've already loaded in place, and a refresh that fails says so without marking the connection disconnected.</p>
      <div class="note-box">
        <h3 class="note-title">A note on MongoDB</h3>
        <p class="note-body">Where SQL databases show schemas and tables, MongoDB shows databases and collections, and the editor takes shell-style queries: <span class="mono">db.users.find({...}).limit(20)</span> runs exactly as written.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="oracle" class="docs-section">
      <h2 class="docs-h2">Oracle</h2>
      <p class="docs-p">Oracle needs nothing installed first. There is no Instant Client to download, no ODBC or JDBC driver, and no <span class="mono">ORACLE_HOME</span> to set — Downpick talks to the database directly. Two things follow from that, and one from Oracle itself.</p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3">Oracle 12.1 or newer</h3>
      <p class="docs-p docs-p--tight">11g and older can't be reached at all. Neither can a server configured for Oracle's Native Network Encryption — <span class="mono">SQLNET.ENCRYPTION_SERVER = REQUIRED</span> will refuse the connection. Ordinary TLS is fine, including Autonomous Database over mTLS, though the wallet has to be in PEM form rather than <span class="mono">cwallet.sso</span>.</p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3">A service name, not a database</h3>
      <p class="docs-p docs-p--tight">Every other engine will tell you what databases it has. Oracle won't — the service is part of the address, so the connection dialog asks for it and remembers it with the rest of the connection. <span class="mono">lsnrctl status</span> on the server lists the services it serves. Get it wrong and Oracle answers <span class="mono">ORA-12514</span>, which Downpick explains and points back at the field.</p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3">Your schemas, not Oracle's</h3>
      <p class="docs-p docs-p--last">The explorer lists application schemas only, so <span class="mono">SYS</span>, <span class="mono">SYSTEM</span>, <span class="mono">XDB</span> and the rest of Oracle's own accounts stay out of the tree. Yours sorts first.</p>

      <div class="danger-box">
        <h3 class="danger-title">⚠ Each statement commits as it runs</h3>
        <p class="danger-body">Oracle runs one statement per call, so a script is split and run in order, stopping at the first failure — with everything that already ran listed in <a href="#running-queries">Messages</a>. Those statements are committed. A five-statement script is five transactions, and a failure at the third does not roll back the first two. This differs from SQL*Plus, SQL Developer, and Toad, which default to manual commit. Downpick has no Commit or Rollback for any engine yet; the confirmation before an unqualified UPDATE or DELETE is the safety net until it does.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="note-box">
        <h3 class="note-title">Two notes on types</h3>
        <p class="note-body">An unconstrained Oracle <span class="mono">NUMBER</span> holds up to 38 digits and the grid carries about 15, so a very large or very precise value can lose precision on the way in. And <span class="mono">DATE</span> and <span class="mono">TIMESTAMP</span> carry no time zone, so they're read in the machine's zone and shown as UTC — a <span class="mono">DATE '2026-01-15'</span> reads as <span class="mono">2026-01-15T03:00:00.000Z</span> on a UTC−3 machine. PostgreSQL <span class="mono">timestamp</span> columns already behave this way here.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="running-queries" class="docs-section">
      <h2 class="docs-h2">Running queries</h2>
      <p class="docs-p">Press <span class="mono" data-mac="Cmd+Enter" data-win="Ctrl+Enter">Cmd+Enter</span> to run the tab, or just the part of it you've selected. While it runs, the results area shows how long it has been going, updating as it goes, next to a <span class="strong-inline">Cancel</span> button — <span class="mono" data-mac="Cmd+Shift+Enter" data-win="Ctrl+Shift+Enter">Cmd+Shift+Enter</span> does the same thing. A query still going after the timeout in <a href="#settings">Settings</a> is cancelled for you.</p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3">One statement at a time</h3>
      <p class="docs-p docs-p--last">In a script of several statements, put the cursor inside the one you want and press <span class="mono">F9</span> — <span class="strong-inline">Query ▸ Run Statement</span> — to run only that one, without selecting it first.</p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3">Being told when it's done</h3>
      <p class="docs-p docs-p--last">A query that takes a while announces itself when it finishes: a system notification if you've switched away from Downpick, an in-app notice if you're still looking at it, never both. Clicking the system one brings the window forward and opens the tab the query came from. Queries faster than the threshold in <a href="#settings">Settings</a> pass in silence, as do ones you cancelled and ones the database rejected — you're right there for those. This is on by default; turn it off in the same place.</p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3">Results and Messages</h3>
      <p class="docs-p docs-p--tight">Rows land in <span class="strong-inline">Results</span>. Statements that return no rows — an UPDATE, a DELETE, a CREATE INDEX, the DDL half of a batch — land in <span class="strong-inline">Messages</span> instead, one line per statement in the order they ran, each saying what it did and how many rows it affected. The total time is at the bottom.</p>
      <p class="docs-p docs-p--last">Both views are always reachable from the status bar, because one batch can fill either or both. Run three SELECTs and an UPDATE and you'll have rows in one and a line about the UPDATE in the other.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="vault" class="docs-section">
      <h2 class="docs-h2">The vault</h2>
      <p class="docs-p">The vault is a single encrypted file on your computer that holds every secret Downpick knows: your connection details, your database passwords, and your AI provider keys. Nothing sensitive is stored anywhere else, and none of it is stored in plain text.</p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3">Where it lives</h3>
      <p class="docs-p docs-p--tight">By default, <span class="mono">~/.downpick/vault.enc</span>. You can put it somewhere else — a removable disk, or an encrypted volume — either on first launch or later from <a href="#settings">Settings</a>, where <span class="strong-inline">Browse…</span> opens a vault you already have and <span class="strong-inline">New location…</span> places a new one. The unlock screen offers the same choice, so a vault carried over from another machine can be opened without editing anything first.</p>
      <p class="docs-p docs-p--last">The path is remembered in <span class="mono">~/.downpick/settings.json</span>, and only once a vault at that path has actually been created or unlocked — a mistyped path, or a file that turns out not to be a vault, never survives a restart. Moving the vault locks the one currently open; the file at the new path has its own master password.</p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3">How it's protected</h3>
      <p class="docs-p docs-p--tight">Your master password is put through a deliberately slow calculation to derive a key, and that key encrypts the key that encrypts the file. In practice that means two things you'll notice: unlocking takes a moment, and changing your master password is instant — it re-wraps a small key rather than re-encrypting everything.</p>
      <p class="docs-p docs-p--tight">If the file is ever damaged, Downpick tells you it's damaged rather than claiming your password is wrong. A backup copy of the previous version is kept alongside it.</p>

      <div class="danger-box">
        <h3 class="danger-title">⚠ There is no recovery</h3>
        <p class="danger-body">If you forget your master password, nothing in the vault can be decrypted — not by you, not by us, not by anyone. There is no reset link and no back door. Keep the password in a password manager or on paper somewhere safe.</p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="docs-h3">Locking and unlocking</h3>
      <p class="docs-p docs-p--tight">The vault starts locked every time you open Downpick, and the app refuses to do anything with your databases until you unlock it. Lock it again from the <span class="mono">🔓 Vault unlocked</span> button at the left of the status bar, or with <span class="mono" data-mac="Cmd+L" data-win="Ctrl+L">Cmd+L</span>.</p>
      <p class="docs-p docs-p--tight">Locking also closes every open database connection, so nothing stays reachable behind a locked vault. It does not clear results already on screen.</p>
      <p class="docs-p docs-p--last">Auto-lock can do this for you after a period of inactivity — off by default, see <a href="#settings">Settings</a>. It watches actual app activity rather than your mouse, so a long-running query is never interrupted by its own idle timer.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="settings" class="docs-section">
      <h2 class="docs-h2">Settings</h2>
      <p class="docs-p docs-p--wide">Open Settings with the <span class="mono">⚙</span> button at the right end of the status bar, or <span class="mono" data-mac="Cmd+," data-win="Ctrl+,">Cmd+,</span>. There are four sections.</p>

{%- for g in settings %}

      <p class="docs-eyebrow">{{ g.group }}</p>
      <div class="deftable deftable--gap">
{%- for row in g.rows %}
        <div class="defrow">
          <div>
            <div class="defrow-name">{{ row.name }}</div>
            <div class="defrow-meta">{{ row.meta }}</div>
          </div>
          <p class="defrow-body">{{ row.body | safe }}</p>
        </div>
{%- endfor %}
      </div>
{%- endfor %}

      <p class="docs-eyebrow">AI providers</p>
      <p class="docs-p docs-p--last">Covered in full below. This section saves each change as you make it, so it has a plain <span class="strong-inline">Close</span> rather than a Save button.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="ai-providers" class="docs-section">
      <h2 class="docs-h2">Adding and managing AI providers</h2>
      <p class="docs-p docs-p--wide">Ask AI needs one provider and one model to work. You bring your own account — Downpick has no AI service of its own and sends nothing until you set one up.</p>

      <div class="steps-list" style="margin-bottom: 32px;">
        <div class="numstep">
          <div class="numstep-n">01</div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="numstep-t">Open Settings → AI providers</h3>
            <p class="numstep-b">Or jump straight there with <span class="mono" data-mac="Cmd+Shift+," data-win="Ctrl+Shift+,">Cmd+Shift+,</span>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="numstep">
          <div class="numstep-n">02</div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="numstep-t">Pick a provider and paste your key</h3>
            <p class="numstep-b">Five kinds are supported, plus anything that speaks the OpenAI format:</p>
            <div class="chip-row" style="margin-top: 14px;">
              <span class="chip">OpenAI</span>
              <span class="chip">Anthropic</span>
              <span class="chip">Google Gemini</span>
              <span class="chip">Azure OpenAI</span>
              <span class="chip">Any OpenAI-compatible endpoint</span>
            </div>
            <p class="numstep-b">Keys go into the same encrypted vault as your database passwords, and are never handed back to the window that renders the app.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="numstep">
          <div class="numstep-n">03</div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="numstep-t">Choose a model</h3>
            <p class="numstep-b"><span class="strong-inline">Fetch from API</span> asks the provider which models your key can actually use, so you're picking from a real list rather than guessing. <span class="strong-inline">Add a model id</span> takes anything you type — a model newer than your copy of Downpick still works.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="numstep">
          <div class="numstep-n">04</div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="numstep-t">Ask something</h3>
            <p class="numstep-b">Close Settings, press <span class="accent-inline">✦ Ask AI</span> in the editor toolbar, and type your question. <span class="mono">Enter</span> sends it; <span class="mono">Shift+Enter</span> adds a line. When you like the query, click <span class="strong-inline">Insert into editor</span> — then run it yourself.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--flush">Working on the query you already have</h3>
      <p class="docs-p docs-p--tight">The assistant can read the query open in the tab, so it isn't limited to writing one from nothing. "Edit this to group by month", "what does this query do?", and "why is this slow?" all work on what you've written.</p>
      <p class="docs-p docs-p--tight">Select part of the query first and it sees only that fragment — and inserting its answer replaces only that fragment, leaving the rest of the query alone. Either way, <span class="mono" data-mac="Cmd+Z" data-win="Ctrl+Z">Cmd+Z</span> puts back what you had. A question that only wants an explanation comes back as prose with nothing to insert, because you asked what your query does, not for a different one.</p>
      <p class="docs-p docs-p--last">It reads the editor only when the question calls for it, so asking something unrelated doesn't send your query anywhere. See <a href="#leaves">what leaves your machine</a> below — a query is not only structure, and a value you typed into a WHERE clause travels with it.</p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--flush">Conversation history</h3>
      <p class="docs-p docs-p--tight">Conversations are kept. Each query tab has its own, so switching tabs switches the thread, and the <span class="strong-inline">history</span> button in the panel header lists every past chat — newest first, each labelled with the connection and database it was asked against, so a question you put to staging is findable from prod. Click one to pick it back up. The same place deletes a single conversation or clears them all.</p>
      <p class="docs-p docs-p--tight">They're saved to <span class="mono">~/.downpick/chats.db</span> <span class="strong-inline">in plain text</span> — not in the encrypted vault. Your questions and the assistant's answers sit in that file readable by anything that can read your home directory, so treat it the way you'd treat a query history file. Clearing the history from the panel removes them.</p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--flush">Running a model on your own machine</h3>
      <p class="docs-p">If you'd rather nothing left your computer at all, add an OpenAI-compatible provider pointing at a local server — for example <span class="mono">http://localhost:11434/v1</span> for Ollama.</p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--flush" id="leaves">What leaves your machine</h3>
      <div class="leave">
        <div class="leave-cols">
          <div class="leave-col leave-col--first">
            <p class="leave-h">Sent to the provider</p>
            <div class="leave-items">
              <div>Your question, and the conversation so far</div>
              <div>The <em class="leave-em">names</em> of the schemas, tables, and columns it looks up</div>
              <div>The query in your editor — but <em class="leave-em">only</em> when the question is about it, and only the selection if you have one</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="leave-col">
            <p class="leave-h leave-h--ok">Never sent</p>
            <div class="leave-items">
              <div>Any row of your data — the assistant cannot run a query at all</div>
              <div>Your database passwords or provider keys</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="leave-foot">On MongoDB, describing a collection samples up to 50 documents to learn field names and types. The sampled values are read outside the window and discarded — only names and types reach the model. Editor text past about 20,000 characters is truncated before it is sent.</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="shortcuts" class="docs-section">
      <h2 class="docs-h2">Keyboard shortcuts</h2>
      <p class="docs-p">Everything below is also on the application menu, apart from the Ask AI box.</p>
      <div class="deftable kbdtable">
{%- for k in shortcuts %}
        <div class="defrow">
          <div class="kbd"{% if k.win %} data-mac="{{ k.keys }}" data-win="{{ k.win }}"{% endif %}>{{ k.keys }}</div>
          <div class="kbd-action">{{ k.action | safe }}</div>
        </div>
{%- endfor %}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="files" class="docs-section">
      <h2 class="docs-h2">Where files live</h2>
      <p class="docs-p">Downpick keeps everything in one folder in your home directory. Nothing goes to a server.</p>
      <div class="deftable">
{%- for file in files %}
        <div class="filerow">
          <div class="filerow-path">{{ file.path }}</div>
          <div class="filerow-body">{{ file.body | safe }}</div>
        </div>
{%- endfor %}
      </div>
      <p class="docs-fine">Your open tabs are a separate, in-app preference and are stored in plain text — turn off tab restoration if your query text is sensitive.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <div class="help">
        <div>
          <h2 class="help-title">Still stuck?</h2>
          <p class="help-body">Open an issue on GitHub. Include what you did, what you expected, and the error text verbatim.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="btn btn--accent btn--help" href="https://github.com/downpick/downpick/issues">Open an issue</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</div>
